{
  "image_name": "0a0c828625dae93a300cbdf125dba4a9.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three classic strategies used to identify cell-surface adhesion molecules and their ligands. In each case, cells or cell membranes are used to find proteins that control how cells stick to one another or to the extracellular matrix.\n\nOne route starts with dissociated retinal cells that normally reaggregate. Antibody Fab fragments block this reaggregation, and membrane fractions are then tested for components that reverse the inhibition, leading to isolation of a specific adhesion protein, illustrated as NCAM, followed by raising new antibodies and immunoprecipitation.\n\nA second route uses hybridomas generated against cells or membranes. Antibodies from individual clones are screened for the ability to prevent cells from attaching to a substratum. Antibodies that inhibit adhesion are then used for affinity purification or immunofluorescence, identifying receptors such as integrins that bind the ECM.\n\nThe third route is ligand-based affinity isolation. A large adhesive domain from fibronectin is used to capture interacting proteins from detergent extracts, and elution with an RGD-containing peptide releases a specific binding protein, again pointing to integrin-type receptors and associated proteins.\n\nOverall, the figure shows how functional inhibition and affinity methods reveal key adhesion receptors and molecules.",
